Z||000000||BOSTITCH||09-16-2000||04:25 PM||||Well basically I can't afford archie's kit so I need to find alternate source for the bell housing adapter piece and flywheel any advice would be helpful. <P>Thanx much. ||||reg||4
Z||000001||pherder||09-16-2000||05:32 PM||||The transaxle to Chevy V8 adapter isn't the big problem ... it is the special flywheel (for manual transaxles) that Archie has Centerforce building for him (and him alone) that would kill the chance of building a V8 adapter kit yourself.  <P>Unless you know someone like Team Python who took a slug of steel and carved off everything that didn't look like a flywheel.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Even that isn't cheap ... they charge $500 to reproduce one.<P>My advice is what I did ... get V8 Archie's basic kit and outsource the rest.<P>Now, IF you have an automatic ... I don't have a clue how/where Archie gets his flexplates from.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Fiero||09-16-2000||05:46 PM||||Actually on the automatic kit you use the four cylinder factory flexplate. There is a spacer that is in the kit to make up the width of the adapter plate. Pretty simple idea actually, but I dont think I would try to make one myself. I think you would be happier buying the cheaper kit also. <P>Thanks.<BR>Scooter||||reg||1
Z||000003||fsrowsell||09-17-2000||01:25 AM||||If you are using an 1985 or earler SBC all you need for a flywheel is a 153 tooth SBC flywheel. I bought mine from the scrap yard for $20.00, 4 years ago and I have not had a problem with my drive train.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||09-17-2000||01:26 AM||||"Unless you know someone like Team Python who took a slug of steel and carved off everything that didn't look like a flywheel. "<P>Pherder, you got a way with words dude!||||reg||1
Z||000005||pherder||09-17-2000||09:39 AM||||Not really ... just sort of repeating what they wrote when describing making the flywheel.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||11
