Z||000032||Zorba||09-25-2000||12:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SCCA FIERO:<BR><B>Everything is illegal in Utah, except having more than one wife    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 09-20-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Now this is what I Call a bunch of B.S. V8 fieros are illegal in Utah, since when?<BR>V8 fieros are rare in utah because the norm here is SUV's and trucks due to the rural,farming culture. Girls here are more into trucks than sports cars, for sure.||||reg||6
Z||000033||Zorba||09-25-2000||01:02 PM|||| Let me just add:<P>It is not against to the law to put a v8 engine in your car as long as it passes safety and emissions. However, if your block is an older one (like mine is), a 68 327, stamped right onto the block itself, 3914638<BR>then you are emissions exempt. Period. You just go to the state to secure an exemption/waiver for your car, period.  IF you put a v8 in it, you just have to go to the state emissions testing facility and you don't have to deal with the 80% of emissions outlets here that don't even have a clue here the engine is. I had one guy opening the front hood and saying:" oh my heck (utah lingo) there is no engine here!"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||FIEROCIOUS||09-25-2000||03:34 PM||||Hey Zorba, not to argue with ya but unfortuneatly here in weber county it IS illegal to install a V8 into a car that never had a V8 option. <P>Utah law states:<P>An engine form a older make cannot be installed into a later model<P>ie. A 86 engine installed into a 87 car. (Not saying you can't get away with it) But if someone ran the engine code against your vin number you could be fined! (If this was the case.)<P>An engine that was not an option at the time of production cannot be installed into the vehicle.<P>Now if you realize what I just wrote that would mean a 4.3 would be illegal just as much as a V8! Like I said earlier I am not so much concerned with emissions laws as I am with ease of testing. A V8 is not a V6 so when it comes time for the visual inspection I'd rather Have six cylinders and have them assume it is a production block than have them count the spark plugs and say "hmmm I can't test this cause I don't have it as an option on my computer screen!"<BR>For those that don't understand, the new testing in weber county has only two options for the Fiero A. 2.5 (4) and B. 2.8 (6) sorry no C. 5.7 (8) It would seem easier to slide by with a six than an eight!<P>Now as for your exemption, That is correct you can become exempt by claiming your car as a "67" or earlier car, however the vehicle in question must have a vin number from "67" or earlier! <BR>With the REAL anal inspectors out their that would be quite hard to pull off as the Fiero has the Vin stamped into the frame in several places.<BR>Other wise that is a possibility! I have an 32 Ford that is registered as an 66 Ford truck because the 32 was an original steel body and had no vin, to make registration easier I took the vin from the 66, <BR>In this case if I aplied for a vin it would be registered as what ever year I registered it and would have to meet the emission requirements of that model year.<P>Unfortuneatly, I take my car to reputable instalations that do infact have a "clue" were the engine is, and also are licenced IM techs.<P>So, I guess I'm screwed?<P>Hey, none the less I'd love to see your Fiero still Zorba!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||HugGRRRFiero||09-25-2000||03:55 PM||||I see one of two things here...<P>1.  If you don't own a V-8 Fiero in Utah, then don't worry about it.<P>2.  If you own a V-8 Fiero in Utah, then consider moving to another state.<p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||elfiero||09-25-2000||04:13 PM||||Archie:<BR>I built my own "kit" without ever seeing your<BR>kit or garys.For a while,I thought about<BR>marketing it as a more expensive alternetive<BR>to your product,(I rubber mount the engine)<BR>I even gave a few to some close friends.But<BR>I don't want any part of this s**t with <BR>everyone flaming each other!besides,MOST of<BR>these folks are dangerous with 140HP!keep<BR>up the good,cheap work-good luck w/the morons<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000037||zeusrekning||09-25-2000||05:16 PM||||I pretty much try to stay out of the flame wars but:Archie you act like you really don't want people making the kits for themselves. I understand.But you get too upset. I'm putting a 455 in my firebird.If everyone else with a 455 in theres got pissed b/c i was doing what they did i'd be screwed.I belive i could make your kit. And i have noticed that even parts you(anyone)make themore you experiment with them the bettr they get. If i copied your kit and made it better would that not be the best thing for the coustomer?I AM NOT GOING TO MAKE A V8 KIT.just to get that straight.If i could save up to buy a v8 archie kit i would but right now the price is just two high .From what i havefrom other fiero entusiast(nonames) Alot of the bigger fiero guy love the power trips. <BR>"V8 Archie" you will always be v8 archie.<BR>if a hundered guys start making the v8 kits you will always be one of the first. If company x makes one that excedes yours would you not try to bring yours up to par? Would you like for the other company to say you are copying them?We are bringing our fiero's up to par with the other v8's some of us can't pop up with the money for a v8 kit.<BR>Hope you don't get pissed at me but he reason i like my fiero is : all the different choices you have, the great people and chances to make new modifications.<P>Ps i would also think it would be great for you two buy those other two companies b/c i seen one of them had very interesting thing i have not seen till last week and you may be able to improve them but best yet try to make the more easily attainable to us guys with daily drivers who want a new can feel or power.||||reg||1
Z||000038||rprevo||09-25-2000||10:55 PM|||| Hey Fierocious,<BR>Is it possible to apply for an Assembled Vin?<BR>In the state of Michigan any time you do so<BR>much as an engine swap Same engine type<BR>your supposed to apply for a new created vin?<BR>Not that any one really follows through with<BR>it but it is a process in place.<BR>I got pulled over with a 68 Chevy truck that<BR>had an 85 HO Iroc block in it and the cop<BR>impounded it and checked all the numbers and<BR>then made me get it inspected by our D.O.T.<BR>When all was said and done I had a vin about<BR>2 miles long. Sorry for being so long winded.<P> Ray,<BR>||||reg||6
